---
// The /huh explainer: how MoQ works, one interactive section at a time, each flagged as IETF standard or not.
// Unreleased (see UNRELEASED in astro.config.ts) and unlinked from the navigation until every section has landed.
import Badge from "@/components/huh/badge.astro";
import Congestion from "@/components/huh/congestion";
import Discovery from "@/components/huh/discovery";
import Section, { type Props as SectionProps } from "@/components/huh/section.astro";
import Timeline from "@/components/huh/timeline";
import Tracks from "@/components/huh/tracks";
import Transport from "@/components/huh/transport";
import MotionToggle from "@/components/motion-toggle.astro";
import Layout from "@/layouts/global.astro";

const standard = (text: string) => ({ text, kind: "standard" as const });
const extension = (text: string) => ({ text, kind: "extension" as const });
const sections: Omit<SectionProps, "n">[] = [
	{ id: "quic", title: "QUIC & Multiple Streams", badges: [standard("Standard · RFC 9000")] },
	{ id: "congestion", title: "Congestion & Prioritization", badges: [standard("Standard · QUIC / IETF")] },
	{ id: "groups", title: "Frames & Groups", badges: [standard("Standard · IETF MoQ")] },
	{
		id: "tracks",
		title: "Tracks: Media & Arbitrary Data on Demand",
		badges: [standard("Standard · IETF MoQ"), extension("+ hang")],
	},
	{
		id: "discovery",
		title: "Broadcasts & Live Discovery",
		badges: [standard("Standard · IETF MoQ"), extension("+ cluster")],
	},
];
const [quic, congestion, groups, tracks, discovery] = sections.map((s, i) => ({ ...s, n: i + 1 }));
---

<Layout title="Huh?" description="An interactive explainer of how Media over QUIC works." bare>
	<header class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 pb-12 pt-14 md:px-8 md:pt-20">
		<!-- Plain text until /quest/m2/huh/routing-and-timestamps.md swaps in public/huh/huh.svg and the cat. -->
		<h1 class="display text-[3.5rem] md:text-[5rem]">huh?</h1>
		<p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[1.1rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
			How Media over QUIC works, from the transport up. Every diagram runs in your browser, so poke at it.
		</p>

		<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-4">
			<nav aria-label="Sections">
				<ol class="flex flex-col gap-2">
					{
						sections.map((s, i) => (
							<li>
								<a href={`#${s.id}`} class="group flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
									<span class="label w-5">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
									<span class="font-medium text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent-ink">{s.title}</span>
									{s.badges.map((b) => <Badge kind={b.kind}>{b.text}</Badge>)}
								</a>
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ol>
			</nav>
			<MotionToggle />
		</div>
	</header>

	<Section {...quic}>
		<p>
			HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one TCP connection, but TCP is a single ordered byte stream. When a packet
			is lost, everything behind it waits for the retransmit, even data for unrelated requests. That's head-of-line
			blocking.
		</p>
		<p>
			QUIC (<a class="green-link" href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9000">RFC 9000</a>), the transport
			under HTTP/3 and WebTransport, gives each stream its own ordering. A lost packet only holds back the stream it
			carried, and the rest keep flowing. Drop a packet and compare.
		</p>
		<Transport slot="figure" client:visible />
	</Section>

	<Section {...congestion}>
		<p>
			Every stream on a connection shares one congestion controller. Its congestion window (<code>cwnd</code>) caps
			how many bytes can be in flight, so each round trip it sets how fast the connection can send. The diagram
			simplifies that to a send rate. When the application writes faster, something has to wait.
		</p>
		<p>
			QUIC lets the sender choose whose bytes go next. MoQ ranks streams: audio above video, the newest group above
			older ones. Opening a stream costs no round trip, and a stream no longer worth finishing can be reset mid-flight
			without disturbing the rest.
		</p>
		<p>
			It starts a little congested, so the bottom stream is starving. Squeeze the window, rerank the streams, or reset
			the one that's starving.
		</p>
		<Congestion slot="figure" client:visible />
	</Section>

	<Section {...groups}>
		<p>
			Video is mostly deltas. A keyframe (<code>I</code>) is a complete picture, and each delta frame (<code>P</code>)
			encodes only what changed since the frame before it, so it's useless without every frame back to the keyframe.
		</p>
		<p>
			MoQ calls a keyframe and its deltas a group, and sends each group on its own QUIC stream. Frames go out one by
			one as the encoder produces them, in order within their group, and each group starts right where the last one
			ended. Audio is ranked above video here, so it keeps up when video can't.
		</p>
		<p>
			When the network falls behind, the newest group goes first, the old group's unsent tail is reset, and the
			viewer skips ahead to the next keyframe instead of falling further behind. Turn skipping off to send video oldest
			first instead, and old frames hold back new ones, as they do with RTMP, HLS, and SRT. (This simulation gives up and
			skips ahead once it falls 10 seconds behind.)
		</p>
		<p class="text-[0.92rem] text-ink-3">
			One stream per group is the single-subgroup case, which is all <code>moq-lite</code> sends (subgroup 0).
			IETF <code>moq-transport</code> also lets a publisher split a group into several subgroups on separate streams.
		</p>
		<Timeline slot="figure" client:visible />
	</Section>

	<Section {...tracks}>
		<p>
			A broadcast is a bundle of named tracks: each video rendition, the audio, captions, and anything else your app
			needs, like a <code>controls.json</code> track. Every track is the same sequence of groups, so a relay carries data
			exactly like media.
		</p>
		<p>
			Viewers pull. A relay forwards a track only to sessions subscribed to it, and merges duplicate subscriptions on
			the way upstream, so a track nobody watches never leaves the publisher. A publisher that watches demand can go
			further and leave that track's encoder idle: if nobody's on a big screen, nobody encodes 1080p.
		</p>
		<p>
			To know what to ask for, a viewer first subscribes to <code>catalog.json</code>, a track
			from <a class="green-link" href="https://doc.moq.dev/concept/hang">hang</a>, this project's media format. It lists every
			rendition with its decoder config and updates live as tracks come and go. A second camera is its own
			broadcast, listed under <code>contributions</code> and opened only when someone picks it.
		</p>
		<p>Pick a viewer or toggle tracks, and watch the uplink.</p>
		<p class="text-[0.92rem] text-ink-3">
			End-to-end encryption is a separate project draft, <code>moq-e2ee</code>, not part of hang or
			IETF <code>moq-transport</code>. It swaps broadcast paths and track names for opaque ones and encrypts every payload,
			so a relay routes ciphertext it can't read. Relays never parse payloads anyway; with E2EE they can't.
		</p>
		<Tracks slot="figure" client:visible />
	</Section>

	<Section {...discovery}>
		<p>
			Broadcasts are named by paths, like <code>room/alice</code> or <code>boat/cam-1</code>. A publisher announces its
			path to its relay, and any session can ask for every announcement under a prefix like <code>room/</code>: what's
			live now, then each change as it happens.
		</p>
		<p>
			That's the whole discovery story. A room's roster is a prefix subscription: no room server, no polling, and no SDP
			offer and answer. When Carol comes online, everyone watching <code>room/</code> hears about it as her announcement
			crosses the relays, and when she leaves, the withdrawal follows the same way.
		</p>
		<p>
			Sessions are bidirectional. Alice publishes <code>room/alice</code> and watches <code>room/</code> over the same
			connection, so joining a call is one announcement plus one prefix subscription. Take people online and offline.
		</p>
		<p class="text-[0.92rem] text-ink-3">
			IETF <code>moq-transport</code> calls these <code>PUBLISH_NAMESPACE</code> and <code>SUBSCRIBE_NAMESPACE</code>.
			It doesn't say how to build a mesh of relays; the <code>cluster</code> project draft adds the hop lists that keep
			announcements from looping. This diagram simplifies: each relay forwards only its own clients' announcements to
			the others.
		</p>
		<Discovery slot="figure" client:visible />
	</Section>
</Layout>
